<GuildGuideLayout title={title} metaTitle="Guild Serial Number Lookup & Decoder | Dating Guide" description={description} canonical="/guild-serial-number-lookup/" structuredData={structuredData}>
<article>
<section class="gu-hero"><div class="gu-wrap gu-hero-grid"><div><p class="gu-kicker">Guild Dating Library</p><h1>{title}</h1><p class="gu-lede">Dating and authenticating a vintage Guild is the first step in figuring out its value. The serial charts are the foundation, but they're only part of the picture.</p>
        <p class="jvg-guide-context">If you're new to this, the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/how-old-is-my-guitar/">guitar dating overview</a> explains what the serial and parts can each tell you.</p><p>Find the serial, choose the right production period, then compare the model and features in the photo guides below.</p><p class="gu-byline">By <a href="/about-me/">Joe Dampt</a> · Published March 12, 2026 · Updated October 15, 2026</p><nav class="gu-toc" aria-label="Guild lookup sections"><a href="#guild-guides">Photo Guides</a><a href="#part1">Numeric Charts</a><a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/">Model Prefix Charts</a><a href="#guild-faq">Common Questions</a></nav><GuildPhoto photo="legacyThunder" alt="Sunburst Guild Thunderbird with offset body, two pickups and multiple controls" caption="A 1960s Guild Thunderbird. The control layout and finish patterns shown here are useful features to compare with the model and serial." eager/></div><GuildDecoder/></div></section>
<section class="gu-section"><div class="gu-wrap gu-hub-intro"><h2>Start With The Serial, Then Check The Guitar</h2><p>Guild's history runs through several factory moves, Manhattan and Hoboken, then Westerly, Corona, Tacoma, New Hartford and Oxnard, each one with its own quirks and numbering. Imported Guild lines need their own model and country context as well.</p><p>Many Guild serial numbers are model-specific. If your serial has a letter prefix, you’ll need to find your model in the era charts below to date it. A prefix identifies a sequence to compare with the model charts; it doesn't make the remaining digits a year code. The <a href="/guild-factory-eras/">factory-era guide</a> explains the transitions, and the <a href="/guild-vintage-vs-reissue/">vintage versus reissue guide</a> separates old designs from later production.</p><p>This guide provides published serial records and physical identification examples. Some records are incomplete, so the decoder identifies uncertainty instead of manufacturing an exact year. If you need help evaluating your vintage guitar, reach out for a <a href="/free-appraisal/guild/">free Guild appraisal</a>. If you're ready to <a href="/sell-my-guild-guitar/">sell a vintage Guild</a>, get in touch for a competitive cash offer.</p></div></section>
<section class="gu-section"><div class="gu-wrap"><h2>How To Use The Guild Serial Lookup</h2><ol class="gu-howto">{howToSteps.map(s=><li><h3>{s.name}</h3><p>{s.text}</p></li>)}</ol></div></section>
<section class="gu-section gu-section--tint" id="guild-guides"><div class="gu-wrap"><div class="gu-section-head"><p class="gu-kicker">Compare The Details</p><h2>Guild Identification And Dating Guides</h2><p>Use the label and model to choose a chart, then compare the features that can confirm or complicate its result. Every photo opens to a larger view.</p></div><div class="gu-cards">{guildGuides.map(g=><div class="gu-card"><a href={`/${g.slug}/`} tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><LicensedImage src={resolveImage(guildPhotos[g.photo])!} alt={g.alt} width={600} loading="lazy"/></a><div class="gu-card-body"><h3><a href={`/${g.slug}/`}>{g.title}</a></h3><p>{g.intro}</p><details class="gu-card-topics"><summary>Topics In This Guide</summary><ul>{g.sections.map(s=><li><a href={`/${g.slug}/#${s.id}`}>{s.title}</a></li>)}</ul></details></div></div>)}</div></div></section>
<section class="gu-section"><div class="gu-wrap gu-article"><h2>Guild Serial Numbers By Era</h2><p>Optimized for mobile. Every chart below scrolls horizontally and stacks into readable cards on a phone, so you can track model years and designations wherever you are. Enter your number in the decoder above for an instant read, or browse the era charts directly.</p><nav class="gu-toc" aria-label="Serial chart eras">{eras.map(([id,label])=><a href={'#'+id}>{label}</a>)}</nav>
<section class="gu-article-section" id="part1"><h2>Part 1: The Early Eras (1953 To 1969)</h2><h3>The Numerical Sequence (1953 To 1959)</h3><p>In the earliest years of production, Guild used a straightforward numerical sequence. The first instruments came from New York, followed by Hoboken production. Guild labels its earliest records approximate, so shared boundary numbers can point to more than one year.</p><GuildTable {...numericalSequence} caption="Early Guild Records: Approximate Ranges And Last Numbers"/><p>The 1959 entry is an approximate last number, not a published first-to-last range. Keep that distinction when checking a late-1950s guitar.</p><h3>General Ranges (1960 To 1969)</h3><p>From 1960 to 1964, Guild continued a single sequential numbering system for all models. From 1965 to 1969, a separate serial system was introduced for each model. Your serial number may be a sequential one or a model-specific one, so check both charts and compare accordingly.</p><GuildTable {...generalRanges} caption="1960 To 1969: Published First And Last Numbers"/><div class="gu-note gu-callout"><p>For 1965 to 1969, the ** marker means Guild does not provide a first number in this table. The listed endpoints do not justify inventing five exact numeric ranges. Use the model-specific chart and the instrument’s features.</p></div><h3>Model-Specific Ranges (1965 To 1969)</h3><p>During this period, refer to the model-specific prefixes to determine the year. For example, F50 serial AD355 falls in the 1968 row. <a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/#models-1965">Open the full 1965 to 1969 chart</a> and compare your complete model.</p><div class="gu-note gu-tip"><p>Tip: the fastest way to navigate these model-specific charts on a computer is to press Ctrl+F and type your prefix into the search bar. You can then jump straight to the rows that share your guitar's prefix.</p></div><div class="gu-photo-grid"><GuildPhoto photo="f50Label" alt="F50 model and AD355 serial on a Guild guarantee label" caption="AD355 matches the F50 row for 1968. The printed Hoboken address needs to be considered separately."/><GuildPhoto photo="f50Serial" alt="AD355 stamped on the back of the Guild F50 headstock" caption="The headstock provides a second view of the serial. Compare both locations when they are present."/></div></section>
<section class="gu-article-section" id="part2"><h2>Part 2: The Westerly Era (1970 To 1979)</h2><p>In 1970, Guild consolidated production back into a single sequential system. Dating Guilds from this period is much easier than the preceding era. Just match your number to the year ranges below.</p><GuildTable {...westerly1970} caption="Guild Numeric Serials: 1970 To September 30, 1979"/><p>The F48 shown below carries serial 74329, which falls between 61464 and 75602 in 1972. A serial in this system is a count, not a month-and-year code. The <a href="/guild-acoustic-guitar-identification/">acoustic identification guide</a> shows its model and construction details.</p><div class="gu-photo-grid"><GuildPhoto photo="f48Front" alt="Natural Guild F48 acoustic with black pickguard" caption="The F48 model and serial belong together when checking the numeric chart."/><GuildPhoto photo="f48Label" alt="Guild F48 label showing handwritten serial 74329" caption="74329 falls in the published 1972 range. The label preserves the model and number in the same photograph."/></div></section>
<section class="gu-article-section" id="part3"><h2>Part 3: Return To Model Prefixes (1979 To 1983)</h2><p>In late 1979, the prefix system was reintroduced. The figures in the <a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/#models-1979">1979 to 1983 model chart</a> represent the last serial number produced for each model in each year.</p><p>An annual endpoint isn't a complete range. Compare adjacent years only when the model and prefix stay consistent. A missing year or changed sequence needs further evidence. Keep all leading zeroes in the number.</p></section>
<section class="gu-article-section" id="part4"><h2>Part 4: Westerly Era Continued (1984 To 1989)</h2><p>During this high-production era, Guild continued model-specific serialization. Match your model to find the last serial number of that year.</p><p>The <a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/#models-1984">1984 to 1989 chart</a> includes changing prefixes and incomplete cells. Artist Award JA100123 sits between the published 1984 endpoint JA100122 and 1985 endpoint JA100127, supporting a 1985 reading when the model agrees. Other rows require their own comparison.</p></section>
<section class="gu-article-section" id="part5"><h2>Part 5: The 1990s Transition (1990 To 1993)</h2><p>Guild continued model-specific serialization through 1994. The <a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/#models-1990">1990 to 1993 table</a> stops at 1993; the next table supplies 1994 records.</p><p>Use the full model designation. Related names such as D40 and D40C are separate rows where the source distinguishes them. Combining them can assign a serial endpoint to a model that has no record for that year.</p></section>
<section class="gu-article-section" id="part6"><h2>Part 6: Expansion And Model Prefixes (1994 To 1996)</h2><p>For 1994, factory records typically provide only the model prefix and the last serial number. For 1995 and 1996, Guild returned to providing full production ranges (first and last serial numbers) for each model.</p><p>The <a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/#models-1994">1994 to 1996 chart</a> preserves those different column meanings. Some published ranges overlap, so a shared boundary number should remain a transition-period answer until other evidence resolves it.</p></section>
<section class="gu-article-section" id="part7"><h2>Part 7: Late Westerly Records (1997)</h2><p>As the 1990s progressed, Guild provided detailed beginning and ending serial-number ranges for each model. This chart covers 1997 production, including several well-known acoustics and the continuation of the Starfire electric series.</p><p><a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/#models-1997">Search the 1997 model ranges</a>. The published table does not supply complete 1998 or 1999 data. For a later guitar, review the <a href="/guild-factory-eras/#corona">Corona transition and later factory systems</a> rather than extrapolating a year from the end of this chart.</p></section>
<section class="gu-article-section" id="part8"><h2>Part 8: Julian Dating And Authentication</h2><h3>The Tacoma And New Hartford Julian System</h3><p>For Guild instruments built in Tacoma, Washington (from February 2005) or New Hartford, Connecticut (from December 2008), a specialized serial system is used. These numbers consist of two letters followed by six digits, for example TJ 037 016:</p><ul><li><strong>Factory Location:</strong> the first letter identifies the plant (T for Tacoma, N for New Hartford).</li><li><strong>Year Code:</strong> the second letter identifies the year. I=2005, J=2006, K=2007, L=2008, M=2009, N=2010.</li><li><strong>Julian Day:</strong> the first three digits give the day of the year, 001 to 365 or 366 in a leap year.</li><li><strong>Unit Number:</strong> the final three digits are the specific unit built that day.</li></ul><p>TJ037016 therefore reads February 6, 2006. NM016002 reads January 16, 2009. Have a serial in this format? The <a href="#gsl-tool">decoder at the top of the page</a> checks the calendar and factory period.</p><div class="gu-note gu-callout"><p>This system is not a universal rule for every Guild made after 2005. Corona, Oxnard and imported models need their own context. Compare the <a href="/guild-factory-eras/">factory-era guide</a> when a format doesn't fit.</p></div>
<h3>Professional Authentication Checklist</h3><p>Serial numbers give you the baseline, but real authentication takes a physical inspection of these areas:</p><GuildPhoto photo="legacyX500" alt="Natural-finish Guild X500 archtop with gold-colored hardware and a Bigsby" caption="The flagship Guild X-500 from the 1960s. The X-500 is still one of Guild's most in-demand archtops with collectors and players. A Bigsby is installed; its mounting and history need checking before calling it factory original."/>
<h3>1. Internal Labels</h3><p>For Hoboken-era examples, look for a label explicitly stating “Hoboken, New Jersey.” Read the model and serial as well as the address. Label color alone is not a date code. When the label and headstock both carry serials, compare them and investigate any disagreement. The <a href="/guild-labels-and-serial-number-locations/">label guide</a> shows what to photograph.</p><h3>2. Potentiometer (Pot) Codes</h3><p>For a seven-digit EIA code, the first three digits identify the manufacturer, such as 137 for CTS. The next two digits identify the year, and the last two the week of production. That dates the pot, which can predate assembly or be a replacement. The <a href="/guild-pickup-and-hardware-dating-guide/#controls-and-pot-codes">hardware guide</a> explains how to use this evidence.</p><h3>Pickups, Bridges And Tuners</h3><p>Compare the installed parts with the <a href="/guild-pickup-and-hardware-dating-guide/#pickup-types-by-era">pickup names and era chart</a>. It separates Franz single coils, DeArmond Dynasonics, compact Guild humbuckers and later HB-1s, with model examples for each. The <a href="/guild-pickup-and-hardware-dating-guide/#bridges-and-vibratos">bridge and tailpiece comparison</a> covers Guild harp tailpieces, Bigsbys, Hagstrom vibratos and the No. 10 Adjusto-Matic; the <a href="/guild-pickup-and-hardware-dating-guide/#tuners-and-headstocks">tuner chart</a> adds Grover, Schaller and other documented applications.</p><h3>3. Finish And Structure</h3><p><strong>Nitrocellulose Check:</strong> vintage Guilds used nitrocellulose lacquer, which often develops fine “weather checking” lines over time. Binding can also shrink or separate. These are condition clues, not proof of originality. Include the neck heel, body edges and any repaired areas in your photographs, then compare the <a href="/guild-vintage-vs-reissue/#modified-vintage-guilds">examples with later parts</a>.</p></section>
<section class="gu-article-section"><p>A Starfire’s <a href="/post/guild-starfire-authentication-guide/">model, pickups and factory-era evidence</a> help select the matching <a href="/post/guild-starfire-value-guide/">Starfire value range</a>.</p></section>
<section class="gu-article-section" id="guild-faq"><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>{faqs.map(f=><details><summary>{f.q}</summary><div class="gu-faq-answer" set:html={f.a}/></details>)}</section>
<section class="gu-article-section"><h2>Sources And Further Reading</h2><ul class="gu-resources"><li><a href={guildHistory}>Guild’s Official Serial Number Records</a><p>Check the published model ranges, annual endpoints and Julian-format explanation.</p></li><li><a href="https://guildguitars.com/guild-support/faq/">Guild’s Factory And Model FAQ</a><p>Read the manufacturer’s account of factory moves and limits in the surviving records.</p></li><li><a href="https://guildguitars.com/introducing-the-westerly-collection/">Guild’s Westerly Collection Introduction</a><p>Distinguish the 1967 factory relocation from the later collection named after it.</p></li></ul></section>
<section class="gu-article-section"><h2>Consider Selling Your Vintage Guild To Joe’s Vintage Guitars</h2><p>I'm a dedicated buyer of Westerly and Hoboken-era Guilds, and I travel the U.S. to make top-dollar cash offers, with prepaid packing and fully insured shipping. If you've used this guide to date your instrument and you're ready to find it a new home, contact me for a free professional evaluation.</p><a class="gu-button" href="/sell-my-guild-guitar/">Sell My Guild</a><p class="gu-return">Dating another brand? I’ve published free serial number guides for <a href="/fender-guitars-serial-number-guide/">Fender</a>, <a href="/how-to-read-gibson-serial-numbers/">Gibson</a>, <a href="/martin-serial-and-model-numbers/">Martin</a>, <a href="/gretsch-serial-number-lookup/">Gretsch</a>, and <a href="/rickenbacker-serial-numbers/">Rickenbacker</a> as well.</p></section>
